Account AC697770 opened on 1 September 2022 and was active for ten days until 10 September 2022. During that period it received only three sizeable inflows: ₹1,90,118 from AC333137 on 2 Sept 00:16, ₹1,43,145 from the same AC333137 on 2 Sept 00:09 and ₹1,20,799 from AC500919 on 8 Sept 10:00 – a total of ₹4,54,062.  

Within the next two days each of those inflows was largely passed on: the ₹1,90,118 and ₹1,43,145 were each followed by one‑off payments totalling 71 % and 94 % of the amounts respectively, sent to two other accounts (the onward recipients are not the focus here).  

The bulk of the account’s activity was a massive outflow to a single counter‑party, AC492085. Between 1 Sept and 8 Sept the account sent eleven large payments to AC492085, each repeating the same three amounts – ₹21,54,153, ₹18,40,321 and ₹10,66,859 – using different payment formats (credit‑card, cash, cheque). The remarks on those transfers read “fees”, “gift”, “loan return”, “school fees”, “trip expenses”, “medical”, etc. The total outflow to AC492085 was about ₹51.99 crore, representing 97 % of all outgoing money and 78 % of the total outflow share.  

The profile shows a very high repeat‑recipient share (0.97), a stable outflow schedule (median gap 1 day, no variation), and no cycles or shared downstream destinations. The money‑out‑vs‑in ratio is 64.6, indicating the account spent far more than it received.  

What was checked:  
- inbound sources – only two repeat senders, no large external funding;  
- outbound pattern – almost all payments went repeatedly to the same account, not to ten different accounts;  
- cycles – none found;  
- common counterparties – no scatter‑gather pattern.  

